{"uid":"4de8dfdbd778a7d2","name":"test_review_verdict_now_pauses_when_the_sidecar_does_not_validate","fullName":"tests.test_run_record#test_review_verdict_now_pauses_when_the_sidecar_does_not_validate","historyId":"1d4bab8bfd0f9c0a2646dfb458b1a599","time":{"start":1791881015337,"stop":1791881015364,"duration":27},"description":"Task 012 (PRD requirement C.3, #219): the inverted sibling of this\n    node's own former self.\n\n    Under its previous name -- `test_review_verdict_is_persisted_when_the_\n    sidecar_does_not_validate` -- this node pinned task 002's original\n    decision for a present-but-unreadable sidecar: the refusal was scored\n    an ordinary rejection, `_step_review` took `rejected_approach_left`,\n    and the run moved straight on to archiving the approach. Requirement C\n    (tasks 010-011, #219) supersedes exactly that run-level consequence --\n    a present-but-invalid findings sidecar is one of the three named\n    `review_findings_unusable` shapes, so the SAME channel state this node\n    has always driven now lands on the discriminating pause instead: the\n    approach stays intact, nothing is archived, no composite PRD is\n    written, and an operator has to look at the sidecar before the run\n    moves again.\n\n    What task 002 actually decided survives underneath the pause,\n    unchanged: `_review_verdict` still folds the unreadable channel into\n    the recorded verdict itself, so the approach's own metadata still\n    reads `verdict: \"rejected\"` with `findingsChannel: false` explaining\n    why its `blocking`/`residual` arrays are empty, exactly as before --\n    it is only the run's OWN next move (pause vs. spend-the-approach) that\n    requirement C changes, not what gets written about this approach.\n    ","descriptionHtml":"<p>Task 012 (PRD requirement C.3, #219): the inverted sibling of this<br />\nnode's own former self.</p>\n<pre><code>Under its previous name -- `test_review_verdict_is_persisted_when_the_\nsidecar_does_not_validate` -- this node pinned task 002's original\ndecision for a present-but-unreadable sidecar: the refusal was scored\nan ordinary rejection, `_step_review` took `rejected_approach_left`,\nand the run moved straight on to archiving the approach. Requirement C\n(tasks 010-011, #219) supersedes exactly that run-level consequence --\na present-but-invalid findings sidecar is one of the three named\n`review_findings_unusable` shapes, so the SAME channel state this node\nhas always driven now lands on the discriminating pause instead: the\napproach stays intact, nothing is archived, no composite PRD is\nwritten, and an operator has to look at the sidecar before the run\nmoves again.\n\nWhat task 002 actually decided survives underneath the pause,\nunchanged: `_review_verdict` still folds the unreadable channel into\nthe recorded verdict itself, so the approach's own metadata still\nreads `verdict: &quot;rejected&quot;` with `findingsChannel: false` explaining\nwhy its `blocking`/`residual` arrays are empty, exactly as before --\nit is only the run's OWN next move (pause vs. spend-the-approach) that\nrequirement C changes, not what gets written about this approach.\n</code></pre>\n","status":"passed","flaky":false,"newFailed":false,"newBroken":false,"newPassed":false,"retriesCount":0,"retriesStatusChange":false,"beforeStages":[{"name":"event_loop_policy","time":{"start":1791880795115,"stop":1791880795116,"duration":1},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"tmp_path_factory","time":{"start":1791880795116,"stop":1791880795116,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"monkeypatch","time":{"start":1791881015321,"stop":1791881015321,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":